<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yalda Night</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yaldā Night, Chilla Night, Chelle Night, Shab-e Yalda, Shab-e Chilla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yalda Night, also called Chilla Night, is an ancient festival celebrated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> of the Northern Hemisphere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, Tajikistan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and Turkmenistan. On the longest night of the year friends and family gather to eat, drink and read poetry, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, late into the night; pomegranates and watermelons are particularly significant. In Zoroastrian tradition the night was inauspicious, and its customs were originally intended to protect people from evil. The name Yalda was borrowed from Syriac-speaking Christians, in whose language it means &quot;birth&quot;. It was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Intangible Cultural Heritage Lists in December 2022.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yalda-night.jpg" alt="Yalda Night" width="330" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vfnyos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L04vb2pizbcs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
